\description{
Predicts an outcome (a measurement, or one of two classes) from a
feature table and picks out the features that matter, in a way that
respects compositionality: the \strong{log-contrast model} (Aitchison &
Bacon-Shone 1984; Lin et al. 2014), fitted with an elastic-net penalty
and the penalty chosen by cross-validation. \code{coda_lasso()} constrains the
features' coefficients to sum to zero, as zeroSum does
(Altenbuchinger et al. 2017); \code{coda_lasso_pairwise()} puts the penalty on
all pairwise log ratios, as coda4microbiome's \code{coda_glmnet()} does (Calle
et al. 2023).
}
\details{
\subsection{The log-contrast model}{

For samples \eqn{i} and features \eqn{j}, with \eqn{g} the identity
(gaussian) or the logit (binomial),
\deqn{g(E[y_i]) = \beta_0 + \sum_j \beta_j \log x_{ij},
  \quad \sum_j \beta_j = 0.}
Because the coefficients sum to zero, multiplying a sample by any
constant changes nothing: the fit is the same on counts, proportions or
rarefied counts, and \eqn{\sum_j \beta_j \log x_{ij} = \sum_j \beta_j
\mathrm{clr}(x_i)_j}. The features with positive coefficients form the
numerator, and those with negative ones the denominator, of a single
weighted log ratio (the "signature"; Calle et al. 2023).

\code{coda_lasso()} minimizes
\deqn{-\frac{1}{n}\ell(\beta_0, \beta) + \lambda \sum_j \left[\frac{1 -
  r}{2} v_j^2 \beta_j^2 + r v_j |\beta_j|\right]}
(\eqn{\ell} the log-likelihood, \eqn{r} = \code{l1_ratio}, \eqn{v_j} the
column's standard deviation with \code{standardize}, else 1) subject to the
constraint, by zeroSum's coordinate descent over pairs of coefficients
(raising one while lowering another keeps the sum at zero). For
gaussian \code{y} the ridge term is divided by the standard deviation of
\code{y}, as zeroSum and glmnet do.

\code{coda_lasso_pairwise()} fits the same model through the log ratios of
every pair of features, \eqn{\log(x_j / x_k)}, \eqn{j < k}, as
coda4microbiome does: \eqn{\beta_j = \sum_k \theta_{jk} - \sum_k
\theta_{kj}} sums to zero by construction, and the penalty falls on the
\eqn{\theta}. It never forms the \eqn{n \times p(p-1)/2} table of log
ratios. With a pure lasso (\code{l1_ratio = 1}), no standardization and no
covariates, its fit at \eqn{2\lambda} is \code{coda_lasso()}'s at
\eqn{\lambda}, since the cheapest \eqn{\theta} giving a \eqn{\beta} has
\eqn{\|\theta\|_1 = \|\beta\|_1 / 2}. With the defaults (standardized,
\code{l1_ratio = 0.9}) they differ.
}

\subsection{Choosing lambda}{

Each fold's samples are predicted from a fit to the others along the
whole path. \code{coda_lasso()} follows zeroSum: the error is the mean
squared error (gaussian) or the binomial deviance per sample, averaged
over folds, with standard error \eqn{\mathrm{sd}/\sqrt{K}} over the
\eqn{K} folds; \code{'1se'} is the \eqn{\lambda} (no smaller than the best)
whose error is closest to the best error plus its standard error.
\code{coda_lasso_pairwise()} follows \code{cv.glmnet()} as coda4microbiome calls
it: the mean squared error (gaussian) or the AUC (binomial; the
binomial deviance, with a warning, when there are fewer than 10 samples
per fold, as glmnet does), averaged over folds weighted by their size;
\code{'1se'} is the largest \eqn{\lambda} whose error is within a standard
error of the best. The default path stops early, as glmnet's does, once
the fit explains 99.9\% of the deviance or stops improving.

Folds are drawn at random with \code{seed} (for binomial \code{y}, within each
class, so that each fold has both). Penalized fits come with no
p-values: a feature's selection is not a test of it.
}

\subsection{Zeros and covariates}{

Zeros have no logarithm: replace them first (see \code{\link[=replace_zeros]{replace_zeros()}}),
or a \code{pseudocount} is added (see "Pseudocount" below).
}
}
\section{Differences from zeroSum and coda4microbiome}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{zeroSum:} the largest penalty of the default path includes every
pair of features (zeroSum's \code{lambdaMax} skips the first feature), and
with \code{given} it is computed from the residuals of \code{y} on the
covariates; the path is always fitted to its end (zeroSum's \code{cvStop = 0.1} stops it once the CV error rises); folds are drawn with ecodive's
random numbers (and stratified by class); \code{max_iters} caps zeroSum's
fixed 100 outer steps. Coefficients agree with zeroSum's to its
convergence tolerance; both reach the same cost to about 1e-10.
\item \strong{coda4microbiome:} zeros get ecodive's shared pseudocount rule
(coda4microbiome adds 1, or half the smallest value below 1, to every
value); \code{coefficient} is \eqn{\beta} as fitted, not rescaled to
\eqn{\sum|\beta| = 2}; the apparent \eqn{R^2} and AUC are computed
whether or not features are selected (coda4microbiome reports 0 and
0.5 when none are); the reported CV AUC is the AUC even when the
deviance chose \eqn{\lambda}. glmnet's solution is matched to its
tolerance, not its iterations.
}
}

\section{Same names in other packages}{

None known.
}

\section{Machine learning}{

Log-ratio transforms (clr, rclr, alr, ilr, PhILR) serve interpretation
and compositional geometry; don't expect them to improve prediction.
With random forests predicting 65 metadata variables in four datasets,
Yerke et al. (2024) found that alr, clr and ilr (PhILR) "performed
generally slightly worse or only as well as compositionally naïve
transformations", and relative abundances did best, by a small margin.
Compare with \code{norm = 'percent'} (see \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}}) before choosing a
log-ratio predictor table.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # Which features predict a measurement? 30 samples, 8 features.
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(240, 50), 30, 8, dimnames = list(NULL, LETTERS[1:8]))
    y      <- log(counts[, 'A'] / counts[, 'B']) + rnorm(30, sd = 0.1)
    fit    <- coda_lasso(counts, y, n_folds = 5, cpus = 1)
    fit

    # The same with all pairwise log ratios.
    coda_lasso_pairwise(counts, y, n_folds = 5, cpus = 1)
}
